1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,080
Thursday night, Timur installs the card zone at the bank.

2
00:00:04,130 --> 00:00:06,840
Halfway in, the install stops.

3
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:10,224
One caller's fraud limit has not been typed in yet.

4
00:00:10,274 --> 00:00:14,856
The message names the missing value, and the caller it belongs to.

5
00:00:14,906 --> 00:00:20,328
In Lesson 5, ecom and the card zone talked only through a written contract.

6
00:00:20,378 --> 00:00:27,912
This lesson shows where every key and setting comes from, and why a missing one stops everything.

7
00:00:27,962 --> 00:00:30,072
Timur could wish for a default.

8
00:00:30,122 --> 00:00:32,496
Here is what a default costs.

9
00:00:32,546 --> 00:00:38,088
Say a payment service shipped with a timeout of thirty seconds, as a sensible guess.

10
00:00:38,138 --> 00:00:45,048
In production, nobody sets the real value, so thirty seconds quietly stays.

11
00:00:45,098 --> 00:00:49,416
One night the card network takes forty seconds to answer.

12
00:00:49,466 --> 00:00:54,576
The service gives up at thirty and tells Malika's site the payment failed.

13
00:00:54,626 --> 00:00:58,488
But the card network said yes, and the buyer's money is gone.

14
00:00:58,538 --> 00:01:02,064
A guessed default looks safe until production.

15
00:01:02,114 --> 00:01:08,048
So what should a service do with a setting nobody has typed in?

16
00:01:08,098 --> 00:01:14,408
That is why I would defend one rule in any meeting: no setting has a default value.

17
00:01:14,458 --> 00:01:21,008
Every setting comes from one locked store, Vault: a safe for settings and secrets.

18
00:01:21,058 --> 00:01:23,432
Three kinds of value go into it.

19
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:28,448
Some belong to the system itself, like which service listens where.

20
00:01:28,498 --> 00:01:35,456
Some are secrets, like passwords and keys, and every one of them is generated inside Vault.

21
00:01:35,506 --> 00:01:40,448
Nobody types a password into a file, and the product ships none.

22
00:01:40,498 --> 00:01:45,920
And some only this bank knows, like the card network's address and the bank's login there.

23
00:01:45,970 --> 00:01:49,016
Timur types each of those once, into Vault.

24
00:01:49,066 --> 00:01:56,600
Before a service starts, it is handed exactly its own settings, in a private file only it can read.

25
00:01:56,650 --> 00:02:01,784
Its own configuration holds only the names of settings, never the values.

26
00:02:01,834 --> 00:02:06,704
If even one value is missing, the service refuses to start.

27
00:02:06,754 --> 00:02:10,688
It names every missing one at once, not just the first.

28
00:02:10,738 --> 00:02:16,808
That is fail fast: stop loudly at the start, instead of failing quietly later.

29
00:02:16,858 --> 00:02:20,456
The same rule covers the bank's business decisions.

30
00:02:20,506 --> 00:02:29,792
Rates, limits and waiting times are the bank's choices, so each is a setting the bank chooses, never a number hidden in the product.

31
00:02:29,842 --> 00:02:35,072
The card zone has its own settings too, and every one is the bank's.

32
00:02:35,122 --> 00:02:41,432
The bank chooses which systems may call the card zone, and which roles each caller gets.

33
00:02:41,482 --> 00:02:51,248
For each caller, the bank chooses which websites may show the card form, how long a card entry session lives, and how many tries a buyer gets.

34
00:02:51,298 --> 00:03:01,832
The bank chooses the fraud limits: how many attempts one card, one group of cards, one address and one shop may make within its chosen time window.

35
00:03:01,882 --> 00:03:10,160
The bank chooses how long an unused card is kept before it is deleted, and how often the keys that lock card numbers change.

36
00:03:10,210 --> 00:03:13,736
And the bank chooses when the nightly clean up jobs run.

37
00:03:13,786 --> 00:03:18,752
So Thursday's missing fraud limit is a real decision, not a typo.

38
00:03:18,802 --> 00:03:22,376
Timur types it into Vault and runs the install again.

39
00:03:22,426 --> 00:03:24,440
This time, it runs.

40
00:03:24,490 --> 00:03:29,984
Settings have no screen, so watch one service start with nothing set at all.

41
00:03:30,034 --> 00:03:31,856
It does not start.

42
00:03:31,906 --> 00:03:36,056
It prints one message: required settings are missing.

43
00:03:36,106 --> 00:03:40,664
Below it comes one line for every missing setting, by name.

44
00:03:40,714 --> 00:03:42,536
Nothing half works.

45
00:03:42,586 --> 00:03:47,408
Nothing listens with a guessed address, a test password or a made up timeout.

46
00:03:47,458 --> 00:03:51,344
Then its settings are handed over, and the service starts.

47
00:03:51,394 --> 00:03:58,424
So a wrong guess never reaches production, and a payment never fails on a timeout nobody chose.

48
00:03:58,474 --> 00:04:04,664
A test password never guards real money, because no password exists outside Vault.

49
00:04:04,714 --> 00:04:12,872
And the bank stays in charge: every limit, window and time in the product is a value the bank chose and can see.

50
00:04:12,922 --> 00:04:16,016
Thursday's install stopped for one minute.

51
00:04:16,066 --> 00:04:21,224
A guessed limit would have stayed wrong, quietly, until it cost someone money.

52
00:04:21,274 --> 00:04:26,768
To recap: no setting has a default, and a missing one stops the start.

53
00:04:26,818 --> 00:04:32,392
So what does a service do when one setting is missing?

54
00:04:32,442 --> 00:04:36,400
It refuses to start, and names every missing one.

55
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:40,240
Next: how every call proves who it comes from.
